rules_version = '2';

// LOAR Firestore Security Rules
//
// Policy: DENY ALL direct client access. No exceptions.
//
// Every read and write goes through the server (Firebase Admin SDK), which
// bypasses these rules. Nothing in this repo initializes the Firebase client
// SDK — `apps/web` and `apps/mobile` have no `firebase` dependency and never
// call `getFirestore()`. The `VITE_FIREBASE_*` vars are vestigial.
//
// LOAR does NOT use Firebase Auth — authentication is SIWE JWT verified
// server-side. `request.auth` is therefore ALWAYS null in this file. There is
// no identity to gate on, so any `allow read: if <condition>` here grants that
// read to the entire internet, anonymously.
//
// ── DO NOT ADD PUBLIC READ RULES ─────────────────────────────────────────
// A previous version of this file granted `allow read: if true` on
// cinematicUniverses, creditPackages, platformConfig, entities,
// entityRelations, universeCredits, and — most damagingly — `content` and
// `profiles` (gated only on a `visibility` field, which is absent on older
// docs and therefore fails open for them).
//
// That version was never deployed; production has been deny-all since
// 2026-03-26. But it sat in the repo as a live landmine: a single
// `firebase deploy` (or `--only firestore`, easy to reach for while pushing
// the composite indexes this project updates often) would have published the
// entire user profile and content corpus for anonymous bulk scraping.
//
// Public data is already served by the server's tRPC/REST routes, which is
// where caching, pagination, field selection, and rate limiting live. Reading
// it directly from the client SDK buys nothing and costs the ability to
// revoke. If you think you need a rule here, add a server route instead.
//
// To deploy rules WITHOUT touching indexes:
//     firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
// To deploy indexes WITHOUT touching rules:
//     firebase deploy --only firestore:indexes

service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    // Deny everything. Server-side Admin SDK access is unaffected.
    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}

// ── Collections ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Deliberately not enumerated here. The previous version of this file listed
// ~24 collections as though that were the full set; production actually has
// 66 — including PII-bearing ones the list never mentioned (`users`,
// `userAccounts`, `walletLogins`, `authOTPs`, `authOTPIssuances`,
// `circleWallets`, `circleSolanaWallets`, `walletLinks`).
//
// That gap is the whole argument for deny-all. A partial map invites
// per-collection carve-outs written against a set that is already wrong, and
// the collections omitted from the map are exactly the ones you'd least want
// defaulting to readable. Deny-all needs no map and cannot drift.
//
// To see the live set: Firestore console, or the REST listCollectionIds call.
